One important point is the link between the parameters (range, marginal variance) and the covariate. Consider the user-friendly parameterization range(s) = exp( some linear predictor ). In this case, the effect of theta on the parameter is non-linear. Therefore changing from -0.5 to -1.5 makes a huge difference on the actual parameters. I added code in the previous script sent to plot the parameter as a function of the covariate to visualize it.

On Thu, 28 Mar 2024 at 23:47, manjagoc <man...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Dear Professor Krainski,
>
> Sorry to bother you again. I studied and ran the last piece of code that you kindly provided me, however, I notice that there is almost no difference between using a smooth or a non-smooth covariate, but this method is really sensitive to prior specification. I mean, if I change the original values of the parameters theta <- c(1, -0.5, -0.5) to something not so close to the prior mean (0), let's say theta <- c(1, 2, -1.5), the coverage percentage drops significantly. According to this discussion <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/Njzmo8M7uHo/m/bre4rNfFAwAJ>, it is better to parametrize the spde object using the nominal range and nominal standard deviation, so I changed
>
> B.tau=cbind(0, 1, 0, proj_cov),
>
> B.kappa=matrix(c(0, 0, 1, 0),nrow=1,ncol=4)
>
> to
>
> B.tau=cbind(logtau0, -1, 1, -1 * proj_cov),
>
> B.kappa=matrix(c(logkappa0, 0, -1, 0),nrow=1,ncol=4)
>
> but I got even worse results. Is there a way to overcome this problem?
> Is the same problem that the authors of this book  <https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/book/10.1002/9781118950203>illustrated.
>
> El jueves, 29 de febrero de 2024 a las 11:41:11 UTC-6, manjagoc escribió:
> > Thank you again for the clarification.
> >
> > El jueves, 29 de febrero de 2024 a las 7:42:10 UTC-6, Elias T. Krainski escribió:
> > > 1. if the covariate does not vary smoothly over the space the variance of the field in your model will not as well
> > > 2. elevation do vary smoothly over space (in the sense that makes sense to interpolate it over the spatial domain). Having the covariate in both the mean of the outcome and the field variance is fine, I've just avoided it.
> > >
